    "post_status": "publish",
    "post_title": "Dan Erlewine - Fretboard Radius Change",
    "post_content": "<style>\r\n  .frc-row {<br />    display: flex;<br />    gap: 16px;<br />    margin-bottom: 16px;<br />  }<br />  .frc-row .looth-figure {<br />    flex: 1;<br />    margin: 0;<br />  }<br />  .frc-row .frc-spacer {<br />    flex: 1;<br />  }<br />  @media (max-width: 640px) {<br />    .frc-row {<br />      flex-direction: column;<br />    }<br />    .frc-row .frc-spacer {<br />      display: none;<br />    }<br />  }<br /></style>\r\n<div class=\"looth-article\">\r\n<div class=\"looth-article-header\">\r\n<p class=\"looth-dateline\">Dan Erlewine</p>\r\n\r\n<h2>Fretboard Radius Change</h2>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"looth-section\">\r\n<p class=\"looth-dropcap\">In 2018/2019 we made a half-dozen Strat and Tele necks from what, to me, was special wood: some very old Honduras Mahogany I had saved for over 40 years, and some spalted maple that I got from the Amish nearby. The necks are skunk-stripe-style and made with a 7¼\" radius. These are still destined for several guitars I am making, but since then I have decided I want them to have a 9.5\" radius for various fretting and setup reasons. These necks have shop-made single action rods — some heel-adjustable, some peg-head-adjustable.</p>\r\nSince the necks have no fretboard, and side dots that are much lower than the fretboard edges, and thick tortoise fret dot markers, there was plenty of room to make it work.\r\n\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"looth-section\">\r\n\r\n<!-- Row 1: steps 1-2 -->\r\n<div class=\"frc-row\">\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/001.webp\" alt=\"Sanding the center with narrow sandpaper on a 4-inch radius block\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">1</span> I started leveling in the center with 120-grit on a 4\" radius block using three different widths of sandpaper from narrow . . .</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/002.webp\" alt=\"Wider sandpaper width on the radius block\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">2</span> . . . to wider . . .</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<!-- Row 2: steps 3-4 -->\r\n<div class=\"frc-row\">\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/003.webp\" alt=\"Full-width sandpaper coverage across the fretboard\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">3</span> . . . to full coverage, in order to “knock-down” the high center quickly, but gradually, and without touching the outer edges of the fretboard until I switched to full width.</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/004.webp\" alt=\"Marking surface and scraping with a radius gauge scraper\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">4</span> I continued my marking the surface, sanding with 120-grit on a 4\" block and also scraping in areas, and end to end with a scraper made from a radius gauge.</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"looth-pull-quote\">The radius gauge scraper trick: sharpen the flat sides with a smooth mill-file and it becomes a precision curved scraper that blends sanding facets beautifully.</div>\r\n<!-- Row 3: steps 5-6 -->\r\n<div class=\"frc-row\">\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/005.webp\" alt=\"Sharpening the flat sides of a StewMac steel radius gauge\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">5</span> I sharpened the flat sides of a SM steel radius gauge . . .</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/006.webp\" alt=\"Filing the radius gauge edge sharp with a mill file\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">6</span> . . . filed the edge sharp with a smooth mill-file . . .</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<!-- Row 4: step 7 solo -->\r\n<div class=\"frc-row\">\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/007.webp\" alt=\"Radius gauge being used as a curved scraper\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">7</span> . . . and it makes a good scraper.</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"frc-spacer\"></div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"looth-section-break\"></div>\r\n<!-- Row 5: steps 8-9 -->\r\n<div class=\"frc-row\">\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/008.webp\" alt=\"Penciling over a small rise on the bass side of the neck\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">8</span> There was a small rise on the bass side way up the neck; I penciled over it.</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/009.webp\" alt=\"Masking tape applied to sanding block to isolate the penciled area\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">9</span> Then I put masking tape on the sanding block, exposing only the penciled area to be sanded.</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<!-- Row 6: steps 10-11 -->\r\n<div class=\"frc-row\">\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/010.webp\" alt=\"Rise eliminated after targeted sanding\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">10</span> It didn’t take much sanding to eliminate the rise.</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/011.webp\" alt=\"Rise completely gone after light sanding\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">11</span> Gone in a minute!</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"looth-section-break\"></div>\r\n<!-- Row 7: steps 12-13 -->\r\n<div class=\"frc-row\">\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/012.webp\" alt=\"Cross-sanding with a full-length radius beam starting on the near side\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">12</span> Then, with a full-length radius beam covered the full length with 120-grit, I sanded cross-wise to smooth in all the sanding and scraping spots. Holding the beam firm and rigid, moving with stiff elbows and square shoulders, I’d start on the near side . . .</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/013.webp\" alt=\"Continuing cross-sanding to the far side and back\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">13</span> . . . and go to the far side, back and forth.</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<!-- Video — full width, outside looth-figure so lightbox JS doesn't grab it -->\r\n<div style=\"position: relative; padding-bottom: 56.25%; height: 0; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 6px; margin: 0 0 16px 0;\"><iframe style=\"position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; border: 0;\" title=\"Dan Erlewine cross-sanding fretboard radius technique\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/feDCvOtWGQo\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\"></iframe></div>\r\n<!-- Row 8: step 14 solo -->\r\n<div class=\"frc-row\">\r\n<div class=\"looth-figure\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://loothgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/o14.webp\" alt=\"Switching back to lengthwise sanding with progressively finer grits\" />\r\n<div class=\"looth-caption\"><span class=\"looth-caption-num\">14</span> When my remaining pencil lines were almost gone, I switched back to a 4\" block going lengthwise, using 120, 220, and 320-grit sandpaper.</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"frc-spacer\"></div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<!-- /.looth-section -->\r\n\r\n<!-- REQUIRED PARTS -->\r\n<div class=\"looth-section\">\r\n<div class=\"looth-callout\">\r\n<h3>Additional Parts Required for This Project</h3>\r\nSome projects require additional parts beyond what can be printed. The following parts or resources are needed to complete this project.\r\n<ul>\r\n \t<li><a href=\"https://stewmac.sjv.io/steel-radius-gauges\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">StewMac Steel Radius Gauges</a></li>\r\n \t<li><a href=\"https://stewmac.sjv.io/radius-blocks-wood\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Radius Blocks (Wood)</a></li>\r\n \t<li><a href=\"https://stewmac.sjv.io/aluminum-radiused-sandingbeams\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aluminum Sanding Beams</a></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>",
